Consider the problem of automatically generating schema-to-ontology (or schema-to-schema) mappings between a relational source database S and a target knowledge base T whose schema is expressed in a formal ontology language (e.g., RDFS/OWL). The available inputs forming the "evidence" for mapping generation consist of a collection E = (C, K, J, D, L), where:

- C is a set of candidate source-to-target attribute/class correspondences (each correspondence is a pair linking a source attribute or relation to a target class or property), which may be incomplete (some true correspondences missing) or incorrect (some pairs spurious);
- K is a set of source-side integrity constraints (keys, foreign keys, functional dependencies);
- J is a set of mined join paths over S (sequences of joins among source relations supported by data evidence);
- D is a finite set of data examples consisting of pairs (s, t) where s is a tuple or fragment of S and t is the intended image in T;
- L is a set of automatically generated example pairs harvested from the Linked Open Data cloud, each pair providing an instance-level correspondence between S-like fragments and target classes/properties.

A "mapping" m is a tuple-generating dependency (or GLAV/GAV view) that, applied to an instance of S, produces an instance of T. Let M(E) denote the set of mappings producible by an algorithm A from evidence E, and let m* be a hidden ground-truth mapping. Define the usefulness of A on E with respect to an evaluation measure μ (such as F1-score over predicted target triples on a held-out test instance of S) as μ(A(E), m*).

Question: Does there exist an algorithm A such that, for a broad class of source databases S and target knowledge bases T, A(E) attains high usefulness μ(A(E), m*) even when the components of E are individually incomplete or contain errors? More formally, characterize the conditions on (C, K, J, D, L) and on (S, T, m*) under which such an algorithm exists, and design and analyze algorithms that exploit all five components jointly (rather than any single one in isolation) to produce mappings that are provably or empirically useful.

